<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Exam 1, Fall 2025</title>

<!-- Include the Exam Instructions file as the first page -->
<xi:include href="../exam-instructions.ptx" />

<page>
<title>True / False</title>
<problem>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            (8 points) Suppose <m>A</m> is an <m>m \times n</m> matrix and <m>\vec b\in \mathbb R ^{m}</m> unless otherwise stated. Select <em>true</em> if the statement is true for all choices of <m>A</m> and <m>\vec b</m>.  Otherwise, select <em>false</em>.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="true">
    <statement>
        <p>
            <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If the system <m>A \vec x = \vec0</m> has infinitely many solutions, then the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly dependent. 
        </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
        <p>
            <m> A\vec x = \vec 0 </m> having infinite solutions tells us something about the number of pivots and free variables that <m> A </m> has.
        </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
        <p>
            <em>True.</em> If the system <m>A\vec x = \vec 0</m> has infinitely many solutions, then <m> A </m> must be at least one free variable. So the columns of <m> A </m> are linearly dependent.
        </p>
    </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>A</m> has a pivot in every row, then the equation <m>A \vec x = \vec b</m> must have a unique solution.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Consider some non-square matrices <m> A </m>
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> False </em>. Consider <m> A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix}, \vec b = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \end{bmatrix} </m>. Then we have the distinct solutions <m> \vec x_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} </m> and <m> \vec x_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} </m>, among many others.
            </p>    
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>T ( \vec x ) = A \vec x </m> is a linear transformation that is both one-to-one and onto, then <m>A</m> must be a square matrix.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                A linear transformation being one-to-one tells you something about the number of pivots in the columns of <m>A</m>. A linear transformation being onto tells you something about the number of pivots in the rows of <m>A</m>.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> True </em>. If <m> T </m> is one-to-one, then <m> A </m> has a pivot in every column. And if <m> T </m> is onto, then <m> A </m> has a pivot in every row. The only matrices with pivots in every row and every column are square matrices.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; The set of vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m> that satisfy <m>10x_1 - 3x_2 + x_3 = 0</m> is a line in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Write <m> 10x_1 - 3x_2 + x_3 = 0 </m> as a homogenous matrix equation.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> True </em>. The set of vectors that satisfy <m> 10x_1 - 3x_2 + x_3 = 0 </m> is the solution set of the homogeneous system <m> A \vec x = \vec 0 </m> where <m> A = \begin{bmatrix} 10 \amp -3 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix} </m>. Since <m> A </m> has two free variables, the solution set is a plane in <m> \mathbb R^3 </m>. (In particular, it is the plane <m> \vec x = \Span \left\{ \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 10 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix} -1 \\ 0 \\ 10 \end{bmatrix} \right\} </m>.)
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>A, B, </m> and <m>C</m> are <m>n \times n</m> matrices such that <m>AC = BC</m> and <m>C</m> is not the zero matrix, then it must be the case that <m>A = B</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Play around with some <m> 2 \times 2 </m> matrices.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> False </em>. Consider <m> A = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}, B = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix}, C = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{bmatrix} </m>. Then <m> AC = BC = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{bmatrix} </m>, but <m> A \neq B </m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>\vec{v_1}</m> and <m>\vec{v_2}</m> are solutions to the inhomogeneous equation <m>A \vec x = \vec b</m>, then the vector <m>\vec{v_1} + \vec{v_2}</m> is not a solution to <m>A \vec x = \vec b</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Compute <m>A(\vec{v_1} + \vec{v_2})</m>
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> True </em>. If <m> \vec v_1 </m> and <m> \vec v_2 </m> are solutions to the inhomogeneous equation <m> A \vec x = \vec b </m>, then <m> A \vec v_1 = \vec b </m> and <m> A \vec v_2 = \vec b </m>. So we have
                <me>
                    A(\vec{v_1} + \vec{v_2}) = A\vec{v_1} + A\vec{v_2} = \vec b + \vec b = 2\vec b
                </me>
                which is not equal to <m>\vec b</m> since <m>\vec b \neq \vec 0</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>A</m> is a  <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix such that <m>A \vec x = \vec b</m> is consistent for every <m>\vec b \in \mathbb{R}^3</m>, then the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                If <m>A \vec x = \vec b</m> is consistent for every <m>\vec b \in \mathbb{R}^3</m>, then you know something about the pivots of <m>A</m>.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> True </em>. If <m>A \vec x = \vec b</m> is consistent for every <m>\vec b \in \mathbb{R}^3</m>, then <m>A</m> has a pivot in every row. Since <m>A</m> is a square matrix, it must also have a pivot in every column. So the columns of <m>A</m> are linearly independent.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>True &#160;  / &#160;  False</em>: &#160; If <m>\vec b \in \Span \{ \vec{v_1}, \vec{v_2} \}</m> and <m>\vec b \in \Span \{ \vec{v_2}, \vec{v_3} \}</m>, then <m>\vec b \in \Span \{ \vec{v_1}, \vec{v_3} \}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Draw a picture in <m> \R^3 </m>.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> False </em>. Consider <m> \vec v_1 = \begin{bmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \vec v_2 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix}, \vec v_3 = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}, \vec b = \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \\ 0 \end{bmatrix} </m>. Then <m> \vec b \in \Span\{\vec v_1, \vec v_2\} </m> and <m> \vec b \in \Span\{\vec v_2, \vec v_3\} </m>, but <m> \vec b \notin \Span\{\vec v_1, \vec v_3\} </m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
</problem>


</page>


<page>
<problem>
    <introduction>
        <p>
           (4 points) Indicate whether the following situations are possible or impossible. 
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>Possible &#160;  / &#160;  Impossible</em>: &#160; Four linearly independent vectors in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Consider the columns of a <m> 3 \times 4 </m> matrix.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> Impossible </em>. Any <m> 3 \times 4 </m> matrix has at most 3 pivots, so the columns of any <m> 3 \times 4 </m> matrix are linearly dependent. So there cannot be four linearly independent vectors in <m> \R^3 </m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>Possible &#160;  / &#160;  Impossible</em>: &#160; A linear transformation <m>T</m> such that <m>\vec{v_1}</m> and <m>\vec{v_2}</m> are linearly independent, but <m>T(\vec{v_1})</m> and <m>T(\vec{v_2})</m> are linearly dependent.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Linear transformations can be thought of as matrices. Consider some different matrices.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> Possible </em>. One example is the linear transformation <m> T </m> that sends every vector to the zero vector.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>Possible &#160;  / &#160;  Impossible</em>: &#160; A one-to-one linear transformation <m>T(\vec{x}) = A \vec{x}</m> such that the homogeneous system <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has non-trivial solutions.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Play around with some different matrices <m>A</m>.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <em> Impossible </em>. A one-to-one linear transformation sends different vectors to different vectors. So if <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> has a non-trivial solution, then there is a non-zero vector <m>\vec{v}</m> such that <m>A \vec{v} = \vec{0}</m>. But since <m>A \vec{0} = \vec{0}</m>, this would imply <m> \vec{v} = \vec{0}</m>, which is a contradiction.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                <em>Possible &#160;  / &#160;  Impossible</em>: &#160; A <m> 2 \times 3</m> matrix <m>A</m> and a vector <m>\vec{b} \in \mathbb{R}^2</m> so that the solutions set of <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> is a single point.

            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Impossible.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text
            </p>
        </solution>
    </task>
</problem>

<p workspace="0.5in">
    
</p>

<problem workspace="3in">
    <statement>
        <p>        
            (2 points) Let <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> be a consistent inhomogeneous linear system where 
                <me>
                        A=\begin{bmatrix}
                        2 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp 3\\
                        -1 \amp 0 \amp 4 \amp 1\\
                        5 \amp 3 \amp 1 \amp 1
                        \end{bmatrix}
                </me>
            <m>\textit{Select all that apply}</m>
        </p>
        
        <ol marker="(a)">
            <li><m>A\vec{x}=\vec{0}</m> has the only trivial solution.</li>
            <li><m>\vec{x} \in \mathbb{R}^4</m> and <m>\vec{b} \in \mathbb{R}^3</m>.</li>
            <li>The solution set of the system <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{b}</m> is parallel to the solution set of the homogeneous system <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{0}</m></li>
            <li>If <m>\vec{p}</m> is a solution of <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>, then <m>\vec{w} = \vec{p} + \vec{v_h}</m> is the general solution of <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{b} </m> where <m>\vec{v_h}</m> is any solution of <m>A \vec{x} = \vec{0}</m> </li>
        </ol>

    </statement>
    <hint>
        <p>
            Options (b), (c), and (d) are correct.
        </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
        <p>
            Option (a) is not correct. 
        </p>
        <p>
            Option (b) is correct. 
        </p>
        <p>
            Option (c) is correct. 
        </p>
        <p>
            Option (d) is correct.
        </p>
    </solution>
</problem>
    
</page>

<page>
    <problem workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>
                (2 points) Let <m>A</m> be a <m>3 \times 3</m> matrix that has linearly dependent columns. Which of the following are true?

                <m>\textit{Select all that apply}</m>

                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><m>Ax = b</m> is consistent for every <m>b</m> in <m>\mathbb{R}^3</m>.</li>
                        <li>the RREF of <m>A</m> has a zero row.</li>
                        <li>the columns of <m>A</m> span <m>\mathbb{R^3}</m>.</li>
                        <li>the system <m>A x = 0</m> has a free variable</li>
                    </ol>

            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                Options (b) and (d) are correct.
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Option (a) is not correct.
            </p>
            <p>
                Option (b) is correct.
            </p>
            <p>
                Option (c) is not correct.
            </p>
            <p>
                Option (d) is correct.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </problem>

    <problem workspace="true">
        <statement>
            <p>    
                (3 points) Fill in the missing entries so that the vector <m>\vec{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 2 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}</m> is a solution to the system
                <me>
                    2x_1 + \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} \amp a \\ a \end{bmatrix}$}$ } x_2 - x_3 = 2
                </me>
                <me>
                    \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} \amp a \\ a \end{bmatrix}$}$ } x_1 - 2x_2 = -1
                </me>
                <me> 
                    4x_1 + 2x_3 = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} \amp a \\ a \end{bmatrix}$}$ }
                </me>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                The correct equations are:
                <me>
                    2x_1 + 0 x_2 - x_3 = 2
                </me>
                <me>
                    3 x_1 - 2x_2 = -1
                </me>
                <me> 
                    4x_1 + 2x_3 = 4
                </me>
            </p>
        </hint>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Solution text.
            </p>
            </solution>
    </problem>
</page>

<page>
    <problem workspace="1.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                (3 points) Consider the following system of linear equations. 
            </p>
            <me>
            x_1 + 2x_2 + 3x_3 = 7
            </me>
            <me>
                -2x_2 - 4x_3 = -6
            </me>
            <me>
            x_1 + 2x_2 + 5x_3 = 11
            </me>
            <p>
                Suppose the system is written in matrix form as <m>A\vec{x} = \vec{b}</m>. Express <m>\vec{b}</m> as a linear combination of the columns of <m>A</m>.
            </p>
            <me>
                \begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ -6 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix} = \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \end{bmatrix}$}$ } \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} + \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \end{bmatrix}$}$ } \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -2 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} + \framebox{$\phantom{$\begin{bmatrix} a \end{bmatrix}$}$ } \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -4 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix}
            </me>
            
        </statement>
        <hint>
            <p>
                <m> \begin{pmatrix} 7 \\ -6 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix} = 3 \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} - 1 \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -2 \\ 2 \end{pmatrix} + 2 \begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -4 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} </m>
            </p>
        </hint>
